    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "The artist; sold to Emile Théodore Delius, Paris, 1877, for 1,000 francs [per see Copie faite pour E. Moreau-Nélaton de documents sur Manet appartenant à Léon Leenhoff vers 1910, Bibliothèque nationale de France, Estampes et photographie, RESERVE 8-YB3-2401, p. 76]; sold to Durand-Ruel, Paris, June 1, 1896, for 3,000 francs [this and the following per Durand-Ruel, Paris, stock book for c. 1896 (no. 3838, as Les Courses), copy of archival reference in curatorial object file]; sold to Potter Palmer (d. 1902), Chicago, June 21, 1896, for 45,000 francs; by descent in the Palmer family, Chicago; given to the Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago, 1922.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Édouard Manet composed this scene of a racetrack so that the throng of horses and jockeys thunders straight toward the viewer. In contrast to traditional sporting artists, who always showed races from the side with the horses in profile, Manet achieved a sense of expansive space with this thrilling perspective. The painting records the last moments of the race as the horses rush past the finish line, indicated by the pole with a circular top.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026032",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Summary",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "<p>This freely executed painting captures the thrilling conclusion of a horse race at Longchamp, located at the Bois de Boulogne on the western edge of Paris. Here, Édouard Manet radically departed from conventional representations of the sport, in which races were shown from the side with the horses in profile. Instead, the competitors gallop thunderously toward the viewer, raising a cloud of turf, their pace underlined by the sweeping diagonals of the racetrack fences.</p>\n<p>In France, horse racing became an important form of popular entertainment during the nineteenth century. Imported from England, the racetrack—with its speed, spectacle, and luxury—was a quintessentially modern space. In Manet’s painting, a throng of fashionably dressed men and women press against the fences to witness the event—one man, at the upper left, uses binoculars to better observe the race.</p>\n<p>Unlike his friend <a href=\"https://www.artic.edu/artists/40543/hilaire-germain-edgar-degas\">Edgar Degas</a>, for whom the racetrack was a constant source of inspiration, Manet produced only two paintings on the theme. Originally a much larger composition, this canvas was cut down to its present scale by the artist.</p>\n",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Description",
          "classified_as": [
